---
// Standalone resource page - intentionally NOT wrapped in the site layout.
// This is the "field guide" handout from Jon's PDC webinar on slide design,
// rendered in the Flourish handout/paper palette.
// Self-hosted fonts (identical on Mac + Windows): Manrope (body) + Newsreader (serif).
import '@fontsource-variable/manrope';
import '@fontsource/newsreader/400.css';
import '@fontsource/newsreader/500.css';
import '@fontsource/newsreader/600.css';
import '@fontsource/newsreader/400-italic.css';
import '@fontsource/newsreader/500-italic.css';
---
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
  <title>How to Make Beautiful, Engaging Slideshows | Flourish</title>
  <meta name="description" content="A field guide to minimalist slide design from Flourish Legal Well-Being - the whole method, no design background required." />
  <meta property="og:title" content="How to Make Beautiful, Engaging Slideshows" />
  <meta property="og:description" content="A field guide to minimalist slide design - the whole method, no design background required." />
  <meta property="og:type" content="article" />
  <link rel="icon" href="/images/lotus.png" />
  <style is:global>
    :root{
      --ground:#E7E1D6;
      --paper:#FEFCFA;
      --ink:#12322F;
      --body:#2B403A;
      --dim:#5C6E67;
      --accent:#F05D23;
      --accent-deep:#D24E1C;
      --glow:#F4824A;
      --peach:#FCEEE9;
      --peach-line:#F2D8CC;
      --hair:rgba(18,50,47,.14);
      --serif:"Newsreader",Georgia,"Times New Roman",serif;
      --sans:"Manrope Variable","Manrope","Segoe UI",system-ui,sans-serif;
    }
    *{box-sizing:border-box;margin:0;padding:0}
    html,body{background:var(--ground);color:var(--body);font-family:var(--sans);-webkit-font-smoothing:antialiased;}
    body{padding:44px 20px 72px;display:flex;justify-content:center;align-items:flex-start;}

    .doc{
      width:100%;max-width:760px;background:var(--paper);position:relative;
      border:1px solid rgba(240,93,35,.38);
      box-shadow:0 20px 60px rgba(18,50,47,.20);
      padding:64px 66px 50px;
    }

    .logo{display:block;width:188px;height:auto;margin-bottom:26px;}
    .eyebrow{font-weight:700;font-size:12.5px;letter-spacing:.26em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--dim);}
    .title{font-family:var(--serif);font-weight:600;font-size:47px;line-height:1.04;letter-spacing:-.005em;color:var(--ink);margin-top:12px;text-wrap:balance;}
    .sub{font-family:var(--serif);font-style:italic;font-size:20px;color:var(--dim);margin-top:12px;}
    .sub-title{font-family:var(--serif);font-weight:600;font-size:38px;line-height:1.08;letter-spacing:-.005em;color:var(--ink);margin-top:4px;text-wrap:balance;}

    .hero{
      margin:32px 0 26px;padding:26px 30px;background:var(--peach);
      border-left:3px solid var(--accent);
      font-family:var(--serif);font-style:italic;font-weight:500;font-size:27px;line-height:1.3;
      color:var(--ink);text-wrap:balance;
    }
    .intro{font-size:16.5px;line-height:1.62;color:var(--dim);max-width:62ch;}

    .hero-inline{
      margin:0 0 20px;padding:20px 24px;background:var(--peach);
      border-left:3px solid var(--accent);
      font-family:var(--serif);font-style:italic;font-weight:500;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;
      color:var(--ink);text-wrap:balance;
    }

    .hr.orange{height:2px;background:linear-gradient(90deg,var(--accent),rgba(240,93,35,0));margin:38px 0;border:0;}

    section{margin:0 0 36px;}
    h2{
      display:flex;align-items:center;gap:14px;
      font-family:var(--serif);font-weight:600;font-size:28px;line-height:1.12;letter-spacing:-.005em;
      color:var(--ink);margin-bottom:16px;text-wrap:balance;
    }
    h2 .rn{
      flex:none;font-family:var(--sans);font-weight:700;font-size:13px;letter-spacing:.04em;
      color:var(--accent-deep);background:var(--peach);border:1px solid var(--peach-line);
      border-radius:3px;padding:5px 9px;line-height:1;min-width:36px;text-align:center;
      position:relative;top:1px;
    }
    h2 .q{font-family:var(--serif);font-style:italic;font-weight:400;font-size:20px;color:var(--dim);}
    ul{list-style:none;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:12px;}
    li{position:relative;padding-left:26px;font-size:17px;line-height:1.54;color:var(--body);}
    li::before{content:"";position:absolute;left:0;top:12px;width:13px;height:2px;background:var(--accent);}
    li b{font-weight:600;color:var(--ink);}
    li i{font-style:italic;color:var(--accent-deep);}

    .slide-example{margin:20px 0 8px;border-radius:4px;overflow:hidden;box-shadow:0 2px 12px rgba(18,50,47,.12);}
    .slide-example img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;}
    .slide-caption{font-size:13.5px;color:var(--dim);font-style:italic;margin:6px 0 18px;line-height:1.45;}
    .slide-pair{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:14px;margin:20px 0 8px;}
    .slide-pair .slide-example{margin:0;}
    .slide-trio{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr 1fr;gap:10px;margin:20px 0 8px;}
    .slide-trio .slide-example{margin:0;position:relative;}
    .slide-label{display:block;text-align:center;font-family:var(--sans);font-weight:700;font-size:12px;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;padding:6px 0;color:var(--paper);}
    .slide-label.bad{background:#c0392b;}
    .slide-label.better{background:#e67e22;}
    .slide-label.best{background:#27ae60;}
    @media (max-width:560px){.slide-pair{grid-template-columns:1fr;}.slide-trio{grid-template-columns:1fr;}}

    .resources{margin-top:4px;background:var(--peach);border:1px solid var(--peach-line);padding:32px 36px;}
    .resources h3{font-family:var(--sans);font-weight:700;font-size:13px;letter-spacing:.22em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--accent-deep);margin-bottom:20px;}
    .rgrid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:22px 40px;}
    .rgrid .rl{display:block;font-weight:700;font-size:12px;letter-spacing:.13em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--ink);margin-bottom:5px;}
    .rgrid .rt{font-size:15.5px;line-height:1.5;color:var(--body);}

    .foot{margin-top:28px;display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;font-size:14px;color:var(--dim);border-top:1px solid var(--hair);padding-top:20px;}
    .foot .mark{font-family:var(--serif);font-style:italic;font-size:16px;color:var(--ink);}
    .foot a{color:var(--accent-deep);text-decoration:none;}
    .foot a:hover{text-decoration:underline;}

    @media (max-width:560px){
      .doc{padding:42px 26px 36px;}
      .title{font-size:36px;}
      .hero{font-size:22px;padding:20px 22px;}
      .rgrid{grid-template-columns:1fr;}
      h2{font-size:24px;}
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <div class="doc">
    <img class="logo" src="/images/logo-orange.png" alt="Flourish Legal Well-Being" width="188" />
    <div class="eyebrow">A Field Guide</div>
    <h1 class="title">How to Make Beautiful, Engaging Slideshows:</h1>
    <div class="sub-title">No Design Background Required</div>

    <div style="height:36px"></div>

    <p class="intro">Most of us were never taught slide design, so we default to bullet points and hope for the best. Here&rsquo;s what you can do instead.</p>

    <div class="hr orange"></div>

    <section>
      <h2><span class="rn">i</span> Keep your slides text-minimal</h2>
      <div class="hero-inline">If you want your audience alive, don&rsquo;t riddle them with bullets.</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Your slides are a visual accompaniment, not an independent source of information.</li>
        <li>If someone could just read your slides instead of showing up, <i>this could have been an email.</i></li>
        <li>Don&rsquo;t ask people to read and listen at the same time.</li>
        <li>Write your slides, then take a second pass and cut more.</li>
        <li>If your deck works as a standalone handout, you messed up.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2><span class="rn">ii</span> How to fake design skills: minimalism</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Minimalism is the only aesthetic a non-designer can pull off convincingly.</li>
        <li>The classic Flourish minimalist slide: one coherent, high-res, full-bleed image per slide, plus an optional short headline.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="slide-pair">
        <div class="slide-example"><img src="/images/slide-guide/slide-05.png" alt="Minimalist slide: headline plus full-bleed image of paint splotches" loading="lazy" /></div>
        <div class="slide-example"><img src="/images/slide-guide/slide-09.png" alt="Minimalist slide: full-bleed image only, no text" loading="lazy" /></div>
      </div>
      <div class="slide-caption">Left: headline plus image. Right: image alone. Both work.</div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2><span class="rn">iii</span> What to avoid</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Collages of disconnected images &mdash; use one anchor image instead.</li>
        <li>Clip art. It&rsquo;s not 1996; we&rsquo;re off Windows 95.</li>
        <li>Background clash &mdash; images that drag their own box of background onto your white slide. Strip it with PowerPoint&rsquo;s built-in Remove Background, though a full-bleed image sidesteps the problem entirely.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="slide-pair">
        <div class="slide-example"><img src="/images/slide-guide/slide-08.png" alt="Bad example: a collage of disconnected stock images" loading="lazy" /></div>
        <div class="slide-example"><img src="/images/slide-guide/slide-11.png" alt="Bad example: clip art icons" loading="lazy" /></div>
      </div>
      <div class="slide-example"><img src="/images/slide-guide/slide-12.png" alt="Bad example: images with clashing backgrounds on a slide" loading="lazy" /></div>
      <div class="slide-caption">Background clash &mdash; each image drags its own rectangle onto the slide.</div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2><span class="rn">iv</span> Finding the image <span class="q">(the hard part)</span></h2>
      <ul>
        <li>High-res, high-quality images only.</li>
        <li><b>Free:</b> Unsplash, Pexels. <b>Paid:</b> Unsplash+, iStock, Getty, Adobe Stock, Shutterstock &mdash; functionally identical, so pick one.</li>
        <li>Skip faces, and above all smiles, when feasible &mdash; posed stock smiles read as fake (nobody is that happy about a salad). If you can&rsquo;t avoid a face, one turned away or mid-activity feels more candid.</li>
        <li>Watch for AI &ldquo;slop&rdquo; creeping onto the stock sites. Filter for uploads before ~2024, or use a site&rsquo;s exclude-AI filter where it has one.</li>
        <li>Stuck for an idea? Search a broad word and scroll through the results to inspire a narrower search.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="slide-trio"><div class="slide-example"><img src="/images/slide-guide/slide-17.png" alt="Posed smiling group - stock photo cliche" loading="lazy" /><span class="slide-label bad">Avoid</span></div><div class="slide-example"><img src="/images/slide-guide/slide-19.png" alt="Woman painting, face visible but mid-activity" loading="lazy" /><span class="slide-label better">Better</span></div><div class="slide-example"><img src="/images/slide-guide/slide-20.png" alt="Just the hand and paintbrush - no face" loading="lazy" /><span class="slide-label best">Best</span></div></div>
      <div class="slide-caption">Posed smiles read as fake. A face mid-activity is better. No face at all is best.</div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2><span class="rn">v</span> Where AI helps, and where it doesn&rsquo;t</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Don&rsquo;t let it build the deck. Today&rsquo;s tools produce well-executed versions of bad slides: polished, but riddled with bullets.</li>
        <li>Do use it to brainstorm images: ask for 20 concepts at once. Most are useless; one usually sparks something.</li>
        <li>Do use it for one specific image you can&rsquo;t find in stock. If the resolution isn&rsquo;t high enough for full-bleed, request the image on a flat white background you can easily remove in PowerPoint.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="slide-pair">
        <div class="slide-example"><img src="/images/slide-guide/slide-22.png" alt="Screenshot of ChatGPT being prompted to brainstorm an image concept" loading="lazy" /></div>
        <div class="slide-example"><img src="/images/slide-guide/slide-23.png" alt="The resulting AI-generated image: a brain surrounded by construction scaffolding" loading="lazy" /></div>
      </div>
      <div class="slide-caption">When you can&rsquo;t find what you need in stock, describe the image you want and ask for it against a flat white background.</div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2><span class="rn">vi</span> Text and readability</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Readability beats everything else. Never dark text on a dark background, or light on light.</li>
        <li>The reliable fix: white text inside a black box, so it reads over anything. Or duplicate the slide and dim the image&rsquo;s brightness on the second slide (right-click, Format Picture, brightness slider), then lay the text over the shade.</li>
        <li>One font is best; two at most.</li>
        <li>Use icons as visual bullet points (The Noun Project, about $2 each).</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="slide-example"><img src="/images/slide-guide/slide-24.png" alt="Icons from The Noun Project used as visual bullet points on a slide" loading="lazy" /></div>
      <div class="slide-caption">Icons as bullet points: cleaner than text bullets, and just a couple dollars each on The Noun Project.</div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2><span class="rn">vii</span> Layouts and motion</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Pick the most minimalist layout your content actually needs: full-bleed image alone, image plus headline, or image plus headline plus a little text.</li>
        <li>Stuck with a firm template? Make the image as big as you can on one side, text clean on the other.</li>
        <li>The advanced hack: instead of a bulleted list on one slide, give each point its own full-bleed image and headline.</li>
        <li>Animation: fades only, revealing points one at a time so no one reads ahead. Anything fancier is corny.</li>
        <li>Video and sound: don&rsquo;t. It&rsquo;s an IT headache, and your slides don&rsquo;t need it.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <div class="resources">
      <h3>A few resources</h3>
      <div class="rgrid">
        <div><span class="rl">Read</span><span class="rt">Presentation Zen by Garr Reynolds &mdash; the bible for this style</span></div>
        <div><span class="rl">Images</span><span class="rt">Unsplash, Pexels (free); Unsplash+, iStock, Getty, Adobe Stock, Shutterstock (paid)</span></div>
        <div><span class="rl">Icons</span><span class="rt">The Noun Project</span></div>
        <div><span class="rl">Fonts</span><span class="rt">Browse at fonts.google.com; pairings at fontpair.com</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="foot">
      <span class="mark">Flourish Legal Well-Being</span>
      <a href="https://teamflourish.com">teamflourish.com</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</body>
</html>
